@startuml HCoreLib
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' HCoreLib - structure only.
'
' Names, relations and the role of each class. No fields and no methods: the
' headers carry those and would only go stale here. What this diagram is for is
' the SHAPE of the library - which class knows about which, and which side of
' the hardware line each one sits on.
'
' THE THREE ROLES
'   «boundary»  something on the EDGE of the process - a pad, a file, a socket,
'               a console, a person's finger. Everything platform-specific in
'               this library is a boundary class, which is why so many of them
'               come in pairs (an ESP32 one and a desktop one) behind an
'               interface, with nothing above them knowing which was compiled in.
'   «control»   logic and coordination, with no state of its own worth naming.
'               Almost every one of these is a static facade: HCoreLib starts no
'               tasks and instantiates no managers.
'   «entity»    a value or a record the rest of the system passes around.
'
' Two classifications are arguable and deliberate. HGpioPin and HButton are
' boundary rather than control: a switch and a pad are what a PERSON acts on,
' and turning that into "pressed" is what those two exist for, debounce state
' machine included. HSha256 and HUtf8 are control rather than entity: they hold
' no data at all, only a transformation.
'
' HLog is drawn with almost no edges on purpose. Nearly every class in the
' library logs, and those thirty arrows would bury the ones that say something.
' Read it as: everything depends on HLog.
'
' Platform-only classes are marked in their package names. HTask, HTaskManager,
' Devices and Portal exist on the ESP32 only - the host build compiles neither
' them nor anything that needs them.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ArrowColor #33556B
skinparam package {
  BorderColor #8FA6B4
  BackgroundColor #FBFCFD
}

' The role is the thing this diagram is FOR, so it is a colour and not only a
' word: the hardware edge, the logic and the data should be separable at a
' glance, from across a room, before anybody reads a single class name.
skinparam class<<boundary>> {
  BorderColor #1F6F8B
  BackgroundColor #E8F4F8
}
skinparam class<<control>> {
  BorderColor #99621E
  BackgroundColor #FBF0E0
}
skinparam class<<entity>> {
  BorderColor #3E7D4F
  BackgroundColor #E9F5EC
}

hide empty members
hide circle

package "Values" {
  class HValue <<entity>>
  class HTimer <<entity>>
  class HHookList <<entity>>
  class HSha256 <<control>>
  class HCurve <<control>>
  class HCurvePoint <<entity>>
  class HSystemUtils <<boundary>>

  note bottom of HSystemUtils
    A namespace of free functions, not a class:
    the platform's clock, thread name and sleep.
    Drawn as a box because everything that has
    to notice time crosses it.
  end note
}

package "Configuration" {
  class HConfig <<control>>
  class HConfigParser <<control>>
  class HConfigWriter <<control>>
  class HConfigPath <<entity>>
  class HConfigEntry <<entity>>
}

package "Storage" {
  class "HFs::HFileSystem" as HFileSystem <<boundary>>
  class HIFs <<boundary>>
  class HIFile <<boundary>>
  class HFsDesktop <<boundary>>
  class HFsDesktopFile <<boundary>>
  class HLittleFs <<boundary>>
  class HLittleFsFile <<boundary>>
}

package "ADC" {
  class HAdcManager <<control>>
  class HAdcChannel <<boundary>>
  class HAdcBackend <<boundary>>
  class HIAdc <<boundary>>
  class HAdcEsp32 <<boundary>>
  class HAdcDesktop <<boundary>>
  class HAdcPinDesc <<entity>>

  note bottom of HAdcManager
    A module of its own rather than a mode on
    HGpioManager: HIGpio speaks in LEVELS, and
    attenuation, calibration and millivolts have
    nowhere to live behind a bool.

    But the board TABLE is still one table -
    HGpioConfig.h declares these rows too, so
    HGpioManager's duplicate-pad static_assert
    can see a divider and a button claiming
    the same pad.
  end note
}

package "GPIO" {
  class HGpioManager <<control>>
  class HGpioPin <<boundary>>
  class HGpioBackend <<boundary>>
  class HIGpio <<boundary>>
  class HGpioEsp32 <<boundary>>
  class HGpioDesktop <<boundary>>
  class HGpioPinDesc <<entity>>

  note bottom of HGpioBackend
    One free function, hGpioBackend(), and no class:
    the seam where the platform is chosen, and the
    only thing a pin needs to reach a pad.
  end note
}

package "Logging" {
  class HLog <<control>>
  class HILogBackend <<boundary>>
  class HConsoleLogBackend <<boundary>>
  class HLogRecord <<entity>>
}

package "JSON" {
  class HJsonDocument <<entity>>
  class HJsonValue <<entity>>
  class HJsonPool <<entity>>
}

package "Lifecycle" {
  class HITickable <<control>>
  class HButton <<boundary>>
  class HBootMode <<control>>
  class HSleep <<control>>
  class HRtcStore <<control>>
  class HAuth <<control>>
  class HOtaImage <<control>>
  class HOtaImageInfo <<entity>>
  class HOtaPolicy <<entity>>

  note bottom of HOtaImage
    **Depends on nothing at all** - not even ESP-IDF.
    It parses the application image layout from its
    own constants, which is what lets the code that
    decides to overwrite a device's only working
    firmware be driven by a host test. Same argument
    that keeps HAuth out of Portal.
  end note
}

package "Tasks (ESP32 only)" {
  class HTask <<boundary>>
  class HTaskManager <<control>>
  class HIWatchable <<control>>
}

package "Devices (ESP32 only)" {
  class HAHT20 <<boundary>>
  class HAHT20Sample <<entity>>
  class HBMP280 <<boundary>>
  class HBMP280Sample <<entity>>
  class HSSD1306Panel <<boundary>>
  class HSSD1306 <<boundary>>
  class HIFont <<entity>>
  class HFont <<entity>>
  class HFont6x8 <<entity>>
  class HFont8x16 <<entity>>
  class HFont24x32 <<entity>>
  class HUtf8 <<control>>
}

package "Portal (ESP32 only)" {
  class HNetwork <<boundary>>
  class HWebServer <<boundary>>
  class HCaptiveDns <<boundary>>
  class HStaticUi <<boundary>>
  class HRestApi <<boundary>>
  class HRestRequest <<boundary>>
  class HRestResponse <<boundary>>
  class HFactoryReset <<control>>
  class HOtaWriter <<boundary>>
  class HOtaPull <<boundary>>
}

' --- Values --------------------------------------------------------------
HTimer ..> HSystemUtils : reads the clock

' --- Configuration -------------------------------------------------------
' Stateless: every call streams the file and closes it again, which is why
' HConfig owns none of these and depends on all of them.
HConfig ..> HConfigParser
HConfig ..> HConfigWriter
HConfig ..> HConfigPath
HConfig ..> HConfigEntry
HConfig ..> HFileSystem
HConfigParser ..> HConfigPath
HConfigParser ..> HFileSystem
HConfigWriter ..> HConfigPath
HConfigWriter ..> HFileSystem
HConfigEntry *-- HValue : one scalar

' --- Storage -------------------------------------------------------------
' Exactly one backend pair is compiled in; the facade is what call sites name.
HFileSystem ..> HIFs : forwards to the\ncompiled-in backend
HIFs ..> HIFile : opens into
HIFs <|.. HFsDesktop
HIFs <|.. HLittleFs
HIFile <|.. HFsDesktopFile
HIFile <|.. HLittleFsFile

' --- ADC -----------------------------------------------------------------
' The same shape as GPIO below, and for the same reasons: the channel asks the
' BACKEND for the converter, not the manager, so the part never reaches back for
' the whole. HAdcManager is the only thing that reads the board table.
HAdcManager ..> HAdcPinDesc : owns the analog table
HAdcManager ..> HAdcChannel : hands out
HAdcManager ..> HAdcBackend
HAdcChannel --> HAdcPinDesc : one declared row
HAdcChannel ..> HAdcBackend : converts through
HAdcBackend ..> HIAdc : the one compiled in
HIAdc <|.. HAdcEsp32
HIAdc <|.. HAdcDesktop

' --- Values --------------------------------------------------------------
HCurve ..> HCurvePoint : reads a table of

' --- GPIO ----------------------------------------------------------------
' The pin asks HGpioBackend, not the manager. A pin needs the PADS, and going
' through the class that hands pins out made the part depend on the whole.
HGpioManager ..> HGpioPinDesc : owns the board table
HGpioManager ..> HGpioPin : hands out
HGpioManager ..> HGpioBackend
HGpioPin --> HGpioPinDesc : one declared row
HGpioPin ..> HGpioBackend : reads and writes the pad through
HGpioBackend ..> HIGpio : the one compiled in
HIGpio <|.. HGpioEsp32
HIGpio <|.. HGpioDesktop

' --- Logging -------------------------------------------------------------
HLog ..> HLogRecord : formats one
HLog ..> HILogBackend : fans out to
HLog ..> HSystemUtils : time and thread
HILogBackend ..> HLogRecord
HILogBackend <|.. HConsoleLogBackend

' --- JSON ----------------------------------------------------------------
' One bump allocator and no heap, so the document owns every node in it. A node
' holds the POOL rather than the document: it needs memory, not a tree.
HJsonDocument *-- "0..*" HJsonValue
HJsonDocument *-- HJsonPool : owns the arena
HJsonValue ..> HJsonPool : allocates from

' --- Lifecycle -----------------------------------------------------------
HITickable <|.. HButton
HButton --> HGpioPin
HButton *-- HTimer : debounce and hold
HSleep ..> HGpioPin : wake source
HSleep ..> HHookList : before sleep / after wake
HAuth ..> HConfig : stores the hash
HAuth ..> HSha256
' Two halves kept apart on purpose: the info is read off the wire and says what
' an image IS, the policy is the device's own and says what it will ACCEPT.
' A UI wants the first without the second, and a test can drive the second over
' hand-written facts without assembling 288 bytes for every case.
HOtaImage ..> HOtaImageInfo : states what an image says
HOtaImage ..> HOtaPolicy : judges it against

' --- Tasks ---------------------------------------------------------------
' The watchdog watches an interface it declares itself, so it never names the
' class that enrols with it.
HTaskManager ..> HIWatchable : watches
HTaskManager ..> HRtcStore : invalidates on a cold restart
HTaskManager ..> HBootMode
HIWatchable <|.. HTask
HTask ..> HTaskManager : enrols itself

' --- Devices -------------------------------------------------------------
HAHT20 ..> HAHT20Sample
HBMP280 ..> HBMP280Sample
HSSD1306Panel <|-- HSSD1306
HSSD1306 ..> HIFont
HSSD1306 ..> HUtf8
HIFont <|-- HFont
HFont <|-- HFont6x8
HFont <|-- HFont8x16
HFont <|-- HFont24x32

' --- Portal --------------------------------------------------------------
' HRestApi and HStaticUi hang their routes on the handle HWebServer owns, and
' the application decides which of them exist at all - which is why nothing
' here owns anything else.
HNetwork ..> HWebServer : starts once up
HCaptiveDns --|> HTask
HCaptiveDns ..> HNetwork
HStaticUi ..> HWebServer : registers routes
HStaticUi ..> HNetwork
HRestApi ..> HWebServer : registers routes
HRestApi ..> HRestRequest
HRestApi ..> HRestResponse
HRestApi ..> HAuth : guards routes
HRestApi ..> HFactoryReset
HRestApi ..> HOtaWriter : takes an upload
HRestRequest ..> HJsonDocument : parses the body
HRestRequest ..> HAuth
HFactoryReset ..> HConfig : erases
HFactoryReset ..> HBootMode : reboots into Normal
HFactoryReset ..> HHookList

' --- Firmware updates ----------------------------------------------------
' Two ways in, one writer. Neither entry path knows the other exists, and the
' rules about what may be installed live in exactly one of these boxes.
HOtaWriter ..> HOtaImage : judges before erasing
HOtaWriter ..> HOtaImageInfo : keeps what was offered,\neven when refused
HOtaWriter ..> HOtaPolicy : builds one per transfer -\nthe two entry paths answer\nthe downgrade question\ndifferently
HOtaPull ..> HOtaWriter : hands it the bytes
HOtaPull ..> HNetwork : joins somebody's router
HOtaPull ..> HTimer : caps the whole attempt

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note as Acyclic
  **This graph has no cycles, and it used to have three.**

  Each one was a part reaching back for the whole to get one thing, and each
  was broken by naming that one thing:

  * HGpioPin asked HGpioManager for the pads. It asks **HGpioBackend** now -
    a pin needs pads, not the table of pins.
  * HJsonValue asked HJsonDocument for memory. It asks **HJsonPool** now -
    a node needs an allocator, not a tree.
  * HTaskManager watched an HTask to read its name. It watches **HIWatchable**
    now, an interface it declares itself, so the watchdog no longer knows what
    a task is.

  All three were closed by a .cpp rather than a header, so none of them ever
  broke a build - which is exactly what made them worth removing on purpose
  rather than eventually, when one of them stopped being merely a diagram.

  **Both kinds are checked, and that distinction is the point.** A cycle
  through headers cannot compile, so it is found the day it is written. A cycle
  that closes through .cpp files compiles perfectly and survives for years -
  it is only ever found by looking. All three above were the second kind.

  The include graph is acyclic **through headers and through .cpp files**,
  checked per type, verified rather than asserted.
end note

note as Diamond
  **No diamond, and nothing to solve.**

  Not one class in this library has two bases, and `virtual` inheritance
  appears nowhere - so the ambiguity a diamond creates, and the vtable cost of
  fixing it, are both absent by construction rather than by care.

  Every hierarchy here is a CHAIN, at most three deep:

  * HIFont <- HFont<W,H> <- HFont6x8 / HFont8x16 / HFont24x32
  * HIWatchable <- HTask <- HCaptiveDns
  * HSSD1306Panel <- HSSD1306
  * HIFs <- HFsDesktop | HLittleFs   (and HIFile likewise)
  * HIGpio <- HGpioEsp32 | HGpioDesktop
  * HILogBackend <- HConsoleLogBackend
  * HITickable <- HButton

  That is not an accident of taste. This library is built with `-fno-rtti`, so
  `dynamic_cast` is unavailable and a virtual base would have to be navigated
  by hand; and it allocates nothing after start-up, where the extra indirection
  a virtual base adds to every member access is a cost paid on a battery. An
  interface plus one implementation chain buys everything multiple inheritance
  would have, and costs neither.

  The OTA classes added nothing here: HOtaImage, HOtaWriter and HOtaPull are
  static facades with no base class at all.
end note

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Both notes above are mechanically checkable, and were checked. Re-run either
' after changing an include, from the repository root:
'
'   python HCoreLib/tools/checkdeps.py HCoreLib        # add -v for every edge
'   python HCoreLib/tools/checkdeps.py HAPLib
'
' and for the diamond half, which is one grep because the answer is "none":
'
'   grep -rnE "^ *class +[A-Za-z0-9_]+ *: *(public|protected|private)" \
'        --include=*.hpp HCoreLib HAPLib | grep -v 3rdParty
'
' Every line it prints should name exactly one base. A comma inside template
' arguments - HFont<6, 8> - is not a second base and is the one false positive
' worth expecting.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

legend right
  |= role |= what it means |
  |<#E8F4F8> «boundary» | the edge of the process: a pad, a file, a socket, a person |
  |<#FBF0E0> «control» | logic and coordination, no state of its own |
  |<#E9F5EC> «entity» | a value or record that gets passed around |
endlegend

@enduml
